Small DTS container samples used by the app's "Test patch" self-check and
play-by-ear. Each decodes to real DTS PCM when the patch is active:
  DTS-in-mp4.mp4      H.264 video + DTS 5.1 (dtsc)   -> qtdemux
  DTS-HD-MA-5.1.ts    real DTS-HD MA (core) 5.1      -> tsdemux
  DTS-HD-MA-5.1.m2ts  same stream, .m2ts extension   -> tsdemux
All three are the FULL-LENGTH sources, identical to ../../restore/testfiles/:
  DTS-in-mp4.mp4      3.837s   2.7 MB
  DTS-HD-MA-5.1.ts    3.877s  10.2 MB
  DTS-HD-MA-5.1.m2ts  3.877s  10.2 MB

Do NOT size-trim the ts/m2ts to shrink the .ipk. They used to be byte-truncated
to 2,444,000 B, which is only 0.874s -- it cut off before the dialogue starts, so
the play-by-ear check played background music and stopped (reported 2026-07-22).

Do NOT re-mux them with ffmpeg either, however tempting the size saving is
(video re-encode + `-c:a copy` gives 3.88s in 1.7 MB with a bit-identical DTS ES,
md5 241ba06f...). Measured on the C5: the patched tsdemux exposes NO audio stream
at all from an ffmpeg-muxed TS -- `gst-discoverer-1.0` finds "audio #2: DTS" in
this BluRay-style original but nothing in the ffmpeg one, and the decode fails
with `not-linked`. Retyping the PMT from 0x82 (DTS core) to 0x86 (DTS-HD MA) does
NOT fix it, so it is not just the stream_type: LG's demuxer needs something else
about the BluRay/HDMV layout. Keep these files byte-identical to restore/.
